Issue: The harness delivers the task's whole demonstration set inside the question, and on LawBench that question is 105,318 characters of pooled case facts: the provider's input filter refused the request outright in 7 of the parent run's 100 samples (DataInspectionFailed, non-retryable, 0 messages recorded, ~10s each), the runner drops a non-retryable sample from its retry queue, and the harness raises the refusal out of the solver on the draw's first call, so a refused sample scored zero with no reply at all — LawBench 0.57 -> 0.51, the run's status fell from ok to partial, and the same four val questions (examples 4, 6, 7, 19) answered 8 of 8 samples correctly two nodes earlier, when their prompts were 1-2K characters instead of 105K.

Modification: The demonstration set is now delivered along a ladder of sizes rather than at one size. `solver/tools/retrieval.py` renders the same examples, in the same order by closeness, at a fraction of the channel's room (`share`: the complete set at 1.0, the closest part at 0.25, the closest examples alone at 0.01), and `solver/agent.py` builds one prompt per rung (`DEMO_LADDER`, `build_prompts`) and carries them into the panel: an attempt the provider refuses is repeated at the next rung instead of ending the sample (`_step_down`), and the rung that answers is kept for the rest of the panel. The patterns that count as a refusal are the ones the runner itself classifies as `content_filter` (`refusal_patterns`, read from the solver config), so no provider string is written into the harness, and an exception that is not a refusal still behaves exactly as before. Every other mechanism (grounding, the vote, `PANEL_SIZE`, `PANEL_BUDGET`, memory preparation) is untouched, and the first rung is byte-identical to the parent's prompt.

Results: {"evaluation_result": {"USPTO": 0.3, "Symptom2Disease": 0.9, "LawBench": 0.58, "average": 0.5933, "status": "ok"}, "message": "The expected effect was achieved: every one of the 260 samples was answered (completion 1.0, `rankable: true`, `status: ok`), and the 7 samples the provider refused in the parent run are all scored and all correct — the ladder fired on exactly those questions (4 of them answered at the third rung, 2 at the second, 10 refusals total) and on nothing else, since all 160 USPTO and Symptom2Disease samples and 94 of 100 LawBench samples ran at rung 0 with the parent's byte-identical prompt. LawBench recovered from 0.51 to 0.58 (58/100 against 57/100 over its 93 scored samples in the parent), the average rose from the parent's 0.5756 to the lineage's best 0.5933, and 7 of the 8 samples that flipped in are the refused ones; USPTO's -0.0167 is churn on a byte-identical prompt (1 sample in, 2 out, 18/60 against 19/60)."}

